WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:07.432
[Gentle music]
This is FsStorefront, an F# app that's running right now. Let's find out where its time goes.

00:12.850 --> 00:20.659
Stop the profiler, and every hot line of F# lights up: the catalog parsing, the basket shuffle, and the discount fold.

00:22.017 --> 00:31.499
The results keep the names the F# compiler gives your code. Every closure and pipeline stage is named after its line, so you know exactly where to look.

00:32.049 --> 00:38.125
Now let's track memory on the same app. SharpLsp snapshots the heap every ten seconds.

00:43.550 --> 00:50.638
F# records and union cases show up as leak suspects. Click one, and you jump straight to the line that builds it.

00:53.183 --> 00:54.974
So, what's keeping them alive?

00:58.379 --> 01:04.974
A module value called sessions, named just as you wrote it. It holds every session the app has ever seen.

01:07.646 --> 01:11.433
The fix: keep the last thousand sessions, and let the rest go.

01:23.613 --> 01:26.126
Rebuild, run it again, and track memory.

01:38.616 --> 01:47.220
This time the heap holds steady, and nothing climbs past Low. That's F# profiling in SharpLsp. Free, and open source.
